WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:09.360
Amniocentesis is a procedure
that lets doctors detect or rule out problems

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.580
with your baby's health
long before birth.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.500
In the uterus
your baby is surrounded by amniotic fluid

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.200
that provides protection and nourishment.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.260
Floating in the fluid
are cells from your baby

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.260
providing doctors
with genetic material for testing.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.600
In amniocentesis,
your doctor will take a sample of this fluid

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.940
to test for specific genetic disorders
and birth defects.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:39.560
Amniocentesis is usually done
in the second trimester of pregnancy

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.240
at 15 to 18 weeks.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.480
It is generally offered to women
with certain risk factors:

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.460
--age 35 or older

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.380
which increases the risk
of chromosomal disorders

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.160
such as down syndrome.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.840
--a previous child or pregnancy
with a birth defect

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.400
--blood test or ultrasound
suggesting a birth defect

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.860
such as down syndrome
 or neural tube defects

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.300
--or a family history of genetic disorders

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.720
such as cystic fibrosis.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.400
Amniocentesis may also be done
in the third trimester

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:13.960
for these reasons:

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.020
--to determine if the baby's lungs
are mature enough

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.560
should early delivery become necessary,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.120
--to diagnose a uterine infection,

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:27.400
--or to check for anemia in a baby
with Rh incompatibility.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.940
To begin your procedure,
your doctor would use ultrasound

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:35.680
to create an image of the fetus
and nearby structures on a computer screen.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.240
Looking at this image,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.080
the doctor can choose a safe place
to insert the needle.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.580
Your doctor may give you an injection
of local anesthesia

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.680
to numb the surface of your abdomen

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.220
where the amniocentesis needle
is to be inserted.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.820
Carefully watching the ultrasound monitor

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:56.140
to avoid contact
with the placenta or the baby,

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:00.440
your doctor will introduce the thin needle
through your abdominal wall

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.680
into your uterus
and into the amniotic sac.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.580
He or she will then withdraw
a small sample of amniotic fluid,

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.100
remove the needle,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.910
and cover the site with a bandage.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:19.560
Your body will make additional fluid
to replace what was removed.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.800
Your doctor
will continue to monitor the ultrasound

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.560
to check that the fetal heartbeat
is normal

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.500
and the baby tolerated the
procedure well.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.260
You may feel mild cramping or pressure
in your lower abdomen

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.240
For about 24 hours after your procedure

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.920
you will be advised
to avoid physical stress

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.800
such as vigorous exercise,
lifting,

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.340
prolonged standing,
and sexual activity.

